  <section class="bg-white py-16 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 sm:px-8" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-ink">Age and fertility: how age affects your chances and treatment plan</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        Age is one of the most important factors in fertility, and it shapes treatment planning
        as much as it shapes the chance of conceiving naturally.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Women.</span> A woman is born with all the eggs she will
        ever have. Their number falls over time and the proportion with chromosomal abnormalities
        rises, which is why conception becomes harder and miscarriage more likely as age increases.
        The decline is gradual in the early 30s and tends to speed up after about 35.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Ovarian reserve tests.</span> AMH and an antral follicle
        count estimate how many eggs remain. They do not measure egg quality and cannot predict
        exactly when or whether you will conceive, so results are read alongside your age and history.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">Men.</span> Age can also affect sperm quality and the time
        it takes to conceive, although the effect is generally smaller and more gradual than in women.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        <span class="font-semibold text-ink">What this means for planning.</span> Women aged 35 and over are
        advised to seek assessment sooner. For women in their late 30s and 40s, Dr. Kamran may discuss
        moving to treatment such as IUI or IVF earlier rather than waiting. This is a medical judgement
        based on your tests and history, not a fixed rule.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

