  <section class="bg-white py-16 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 sm:px-8" data-reveal>
      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-ink">What if your fertility tests are normal? Understanding unexplained infertility</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        Hearing that your tests are normal can be confusing when pregnancy has not happened. Normal
        results are reassuring, but they do not mean nothing is wrong. Standard tests check the
        common causes: ovulation, sperm, the uterus and the fallopian tubes. They cannot assess
        everything, such as egg quality or how well egg and sperm interact.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        When no cause is found after a standard assessment, the term is unexplained infertility.
        It is common, and it does not mean treatment cannot help. Next steps depend mainly on your
        age and how long you have been trying:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 leading-relaxed text-body">
        <li>Younger couples who have been trying for a shorter time may reasonably continue trying for a period with guidance, such as ovulation tracking and timing.</li>
        <li>Couples with a longer history, or where the woman is older, may be offered treatment sooner, such as ovulation induction with IUI, or IVF.</li>
        <li>A repeat or extended test may be advised if your history suggests something was missed. A single semen result can differ from the next.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        Dr. Kamran explains the reasoning for whichever path he suggests, and you are welcome to
        ask for a second opinion.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-cloud py-16 md:py-20">
