      <h2 class="font-heading text-xl font-bold text-ink">Fertility help after a miscarriage or repeated pregnancy loss</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        Miscarriage is common, and a single miscarriage rarely needs an extensive fertility
        work-up. It is still a loss, and it is reasonable to ask for advice.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        Specialist guidelines differ on when to investigate repeated loss. Some advise assessment
        after two consecutive miscarriages and others after three, and earlier assessment may be
        appropriate if you are 35 or older or the loss happened later in pregnancy.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        An assessment usually starts with your history and may include an ultrasound of the uterus,
        thyroid and blood sugar tests, and other blood tests chosen from your history. Tests for
        the couple, such as chromosomal testing, are used only in selected cases. Many couples who
        have had losses go on to have a healthy pregnancy. Where a problem sits outside fertility
        care, Dr. Kamran will say so and advise on referral.
        {links.loss && <> <a href={links.loss} class="text-azure underline hover:text-navy">Read more about pregnancy loss →</a></>}
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-body">
        If you are pregnant and have heavy bleeding or severe pain, seek urgent medical care rather
        than waiting for an appointment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

